// Redundant write after read elimination: the transformed program, with the
// write x := r1 removed.
//
// Source: Ševčík, "Program Transformations in Weak Memory Models" (PhD thesis,
//         Edinburgh 2008), §5.3.1.
//
// The JMM ALLOWS r1 = 1, r2 = 2 here, though it forbids it in the source
// program ("Redundant Write after Read Elimination.lit"), which is why the
// thesis counts the transformation invalid. The first two threads race on x
// under different locks, and the third, holding both, sees both writes
// happen before its reads, with neither overwriting the other in hb.
//
// sMRD FORBIDS it, as any coherent model does: x := 1 and x := 2 are ordered by
// co, both happen before r1 := x, and reading the co-earlier one there and the
// co-later one at r2 := x is fine only if the co-later one did not happen
// before r1 := x. Jeffrey & Riely ("On Thin Air Reads", LMCS 2018) note the
// same: this counterexample applies to any sensible non-coherent semantics.
//
x := 0;
{
  lock m1;
  x := 2;
  unlock m1;
} ||| {{
  lock m2;
  x := 1;
  unlock m2;
} ||| {
  lock m1;
    lock m2;
      r1 := x;
      r2 := x;
    unlock m2;
  unlock m1;
}}
%%
forbid (r1 = 1 && r2 = 2) [Sevcik]
